The hairlines are fixed and recaptured in both themes; the new crops are above. Nothing is committed.

**The fix** ([HeaderControls.cpp](src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Shell/HeaderControls.cpp)): the menu used a standard Windows line control, which ignores the colour it is given. It now draws each hairline as a thin filled strip:
- the subtle border colour from the tokens;
- 1 DIP thick, the same width as the menu's outline;
- 12 DIP in from each side, so it lines up with the row labels;
- a window name, which the harness check that looked for the old line control now uses instead.

The fix is in the menu code every menu shares, so the plate, object, printer and filament menus get the same hairline.

**Measured in the new captures:** the hairlines are exactly #E8DFEB in light and #4B3E57 in dark, the token values. They are 1 pixel thick in both themes; dark was 2 before.

**Tests:**
- **Light and dark captures:** both passed with no failures.
- **Default harness:** the first run had six extra failures, in the filament menu's keyboard checks. Your real mouse pointer was resting where the filament menu opens, so it hovered over a row and took the keyboard selection away. I moved the pointer to the bottom-right corner of the screen and reran. Only the six known Windows failures remain, and all the menu, filament and separator checks pass.

**Not changed:** the board's other small differences, which you didn't ask for:
- the menu opens about 10 DIP higher than on the board;
- there is no drop shadow;
- there is 9 DIP of space around each hairline where the board has 13.

I also added a note to memory about parking the pointer before a harness run, and deleted this run's WebView2 folders.

